  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nThe cold front has made its way into the South Texas region this \nmorning, with strong winds coming out of the north from behind the \nfront. Gust's today are expected to be up around 25-35 knots, with \nsome areas possibly getting up to 40 knots closer offshore. A wind \nadvisory remains in effect through this morning and into this \nafternoon. Those high winds won't be too much of a concern as they \nwill begin to die down tomorrow afternoon, allowing for a beautiful \nweekend with mostly clear skies and cooler temperatures. \n\nTomorrow morning will be significantly cooler than normal due to the \nfront with highs in the low 70's across the South Texas region. \nExpect lows to dip into the mid to high 50's tonight and even  \nSaturday night as well. Unfortunately, these cool conditions won't\nlast for too long as we can see temperatures increasing starting \nearly next week with temperatures reaching into the high 80's and \nlow 90's over the CWA.\n\nMost of the rain chances with this frontal passage will remain \noffshore with a slight chance of showers early this afternoon but \nwill ultimately clear up as relatively dry air filters in from the \nfront. Humidity will be low in most areas across the South Texas \nregion, especially in the evenings this weekend for the Brush \nCountry. \n\nCoastal flooding will also be a concern from the strong northerly \nwinds with swell periods ranging from 7 to 8 seconds. A coastal \nflood advisory will remain in effect through Sunday morning, with \nthe biggest concern being during high tide. Rip current risk will \nalso be high through Saturday due to the long swell period.",
